{
  "id": "proj-cob-pizza-oven",
  "slug": "cob-pizza-oven",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Cob Pizza Oven",
  "summary": "A wood-fired dome of clay, sand and straw on a block plinth. Hits 800 F, costs almost nothing.",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedHours": 30,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Dig a 4 ft square 6 in deep and compact 4 in of gravel into it with the tamper. Dry-stack concrete blocks three courses high in a square ring and fill the centre with compacted gravel and rubble.",
    "Set 2x4 forms around the top, lay a rebar grid tied with wire, and pour a 4 in concrete slab. Keep it damp under a tarp for a week.",
    "Spread a 4 in insulation layer of perlite bound with clay slip (about 5 parts perlite to 1 part slip by volume) across the slab, and let it firm up for a day or two.",
    "Lay a 1/2 in bed of sand mixed with a little fire clay and set the firebricks tight, without mortar, tapping each level with the rubber mallet. This is the hearth.",
    "Build a damp-sand dome on the hearth about 27 in across and 16 in high, then cover it in wet newspaper so it releases later. Keep the door height at about 63% of the dome height (10 in).",
    "Mix the thermal layer from fire clay and sand (about 1 to 2.5, adjusted until a rolled sausage bends without cracking), stamping it with water on the tarp. Pack a 3 in layer over the form with no straw in it, because straw burns out next to the fire.",
    "After one to three days cut the door, scoop out the sand and run small drying fires for a week, checking hearth temperature with the infrared thermometer. Add a 3 in outer insulating layer of clay slip mixed with chopped straw, then a thin plaster coat. Keep the oven under a roof or a fitted tarp; rain dissolves cob."
  ],
